{"page":142,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.20386,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"128 Chapter 3: Derivatives"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.09093,"w":0.33363,"h":0.03581,"fs":0.03581,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"3.2 The Derivative as a Function"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.1362,"w":0.58828,"h":0.01499,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"In the last section we defined the derivative of y = ƒ(x) at the point x = x0 to be the limit"},{"x":0.59893,"y":0.1649,"w":0.12005,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(x0 + h) - ƒ(x0)"},{"x":0.0585,"y":0.168,"w":0.12123,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"HISTORICAL ESSAY"},{"x":0.5108,"y":0.17457,"w":0.0845,"h":0.01499,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ′(x0) = lim"},{"x":0.65486,"y":0.17526,"w":0.06991,"h":0.02006,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h ."},{"x":0.0585,"y":0.18347,"w":0.08742,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The Derivative"},{"x":0.57218,"y":0.185,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.20593,"w":0.59299,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"We now investigate the derivative as a function derived from ƒ by considering the limit at each point x in the domain of ƒ."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.28487,"w":0.53247,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"DEFINITION The derivative of the function ƒ(x) with respect to the variable x is the function ƒ′ whose value at x is"},{"x":0.60158,"y":0.3218,"w":0.10943,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(x + h) - ƒ(x)"},{"x":0.51876,"y":0.33129,"w":0.07919,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ′(x) = lim"},{"x":0.65221,"y":0.33197,"w":0.06461,"h":0.02006,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h ,"},{"x":0.57484,"y":0.34172,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.35358,"y":0.36267,"w":0.1622,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"provided the limit exists."},{"x":0.1837,"y":0.40202,"w":0.04273,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = f(x)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.42106,"w":0.59308,"h":0.08954,"fs":0.01279,"n":6,"k":1,"t":"We use the notation ƒ(x) in the definition to emphasize the independent variable x with respect to which the derivative function ƒ′(x) is being defined. The domain of ƒ′ is the set of points in the domain of ƒ for which the limit exists, which means that the domain may be the same as or smaller than the domain of ƒ. If ƒ′ exists at a particular x, we say that ƒ is differentiable (has a derivative) at x. If ƒ′ exists at every point in the domain of ƒ, we call ƒ differentiable."},{"x":0.2191,"y":0.42404,"w":0.07779,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Secant slope is"},{"x":0.22706,"y":0.43738,"w":0.05567,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"f(z) −f(x)"},{"x":0.24072,"y":0.44853,"w":0.02835,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"z −x"},{"x":0.12704,"y":0.45218,"w":0.04921,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Q(z, f(z))"},{"x":0.18836,"y":0.48573,"w":0.05567,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"f(z) −f(x)"},{"x":0.05506,"y":0.4908,"w":0.04919,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P(x, f(x))"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.51248,"w":0.5931,"h":0.05951,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"If we write z = x + h, then h = z - x and h approaches 0 if and only if z approaches x. Therefore, an equivalent definition of the derivative is as follows (see Figure 3.4). This formula is sometimes more convenient to use when finding a derivative function, and focuses on the point z that approaches x."},{"x":0.11198,"y":0.52662,"w":0.05234,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"h = z −x"},{"x":0.09415,"y":0.55835,"w":0.13712,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"x z = x + h"},{"x":0.06611,"y":0.5761,"w":0.10961,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Derivative of f at x is"},{"x":0.14564,"y":0.58753,"w":0.08038,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"f(x + h) −f(x)"},{"x":0.07966,"y":0.59452,"w":0.0575,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"f'(x) = lim"},{"x":0.12026,"y":0.60428,"w":0.02071,"h":0.00808,"fs":0.00767,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"h:0"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.62063,"w":0.26963,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Alternative Formula for the Derivative"},{"x":0.14597,"y":0.62298,"w":0.05567,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"f(z) −f(x)"},{"x":0.10553,"y":0.62997,"w":0.03163,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.15964,"y":0.63414,"w":0.02835,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"z −x"},{"x":0.12026,"y":0.63973,"w":0.01966,"h":0.00807,"fs":0.00767,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"z:x"},{"x":0.62017,"y":0.64028,"w":0.07607,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(z) - ƒ(x)"},{"x":0.53925,"y":0.64977,"w":0.07825,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ′(x) = lim"},{"x":0.63918,"y":0.65403,"w":0.038,"h":0.01346,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"z - x"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.65939,"w":0.24287,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.4 Two forms for the differ- ence quotient."},{"x":0.59532,"y":0.66019,"w":0.02263,"h":0.01038,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"zSx"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.71583,"w":0.32531,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Calculating Derivatives from the Definition"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.74046,"w":0.593,"h":0.02816,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The process of calculating a derivative is called differentiation. To emphasize the idea that differentiation is an operation performed on a function y = ƒ(x), we use the notation"},{"x":0.5959,"y":0.78225,"w":0.04384,"h":0.02727,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"d dxƒ(x)"},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.82332,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"as another way to denote the derivative ƒ′(x). Example 1 of Section 3.1 illustrated the dif- ferentiation process for the function y = 1>x when x = a. For x representing any point in the domain, we get the formula"},{"x":0.05789,"y":0.84774,"w":0.23635,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Derivative of the Reciprocal Function"},{"x":0.10795,"y":0.86944,"w":0.03453,"h":0.02768,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"d dx a1"},{"x":0.13554,"y":0.86944,"w":0.06012,"h":0.0249,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xb = - 1"},{"x":0.56492,"y":0.87789,"w":0.03844,"h":0.0305,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"d dx a1"},{"x":0.59565,"y":0.87789,"w":0.06678,"h":0.02747,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xb = - 1"},{"x":0.18601,"y":0.87823,"w":0.06732,"h":0.02039,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"x2, x ≠0"},{"x":0.65175,"y":0.88833,"w":0.01899,"h":0.02167,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x2."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.92284,"w":0.55277,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Here are two more examples in which we allow x to be any point in the domain of ƒ."}]}